## Why I Built This
Blueprint's UI was being reviewed informally and late — "does this look OK?" after the code was already written. Problems were caught post-implementation, fixes were expensive, and no review was systematic across user types. This skill runs a structured critique against all five personas before any component is considered shippable, catching issues when they're cheap to fix.

## Methodology
The skill evaluates against five criteria in sequence: persona fit, information hierarchy, state coverage, visual consistency (design system compliance), and product value. The five-persona framework — Student, Working Professional, CXO/Founder, Builder, Other — forces evaluation across a wide range of technical comfort and budget sensitivity. State coverage is checked explicitly because empty, loading, and error states are the most commonly skipped during development.

## How to Use
Trigger: "Review this UI" / "Critique this component" / auto-triggers after writing any visible .tsx file
Inputs needed: Component name or screen description; React/TSX code or screenshot for CSS variable compliance checks; primary persona audience if known.

## What Good Output Looks Like
A gate report with per-persona observations, flagged hierarchy issues, all five state checks, specific CSS variable violations rather than general style notes, and a clear SHIP IT / MINOR FIXES / REWORK verdict — followed by one to three "What I'd do next" product suggestions that think beyond the component being reviewed.

## Known Failure Modes
The skill can over-index on CXO/Founder concerns for screens that are genuinely builder-facing — primary persona context matters. CSS variable compliance checks require the actual code; description-only input cannot catch hardcoded hex values.
